7.4 seconds from form submission to a scored lead, CRM record, routed reply and owner alert.

My Speed-to-Lead workflow runs across nine n8n nodes.

## A score needs a reason

The workflow validates the form, then scores the lead against a fixed rubric.

The model returns a number with a stated reason. One logged test produced 9 out of 10 with its reasoning attached.

## Routing follows the score

Hot and nurture leads follow separate paths. Each path receives a tailored reply and creates an owner alert.

The business keeps final qualification and pricing decisions.

## The CRM write happens first

Both send paths depend on the database node. If email delivery fails, the lead record still exists.

The dependency lives in the graph rather than the visual placement of nodes.

## The number comes from the final order

The 7.4-second result belongs to the logged execution with the final CRM-first structure. It is not an estimate or an earlier run.

Fast follow-up earns attention. Durable capture protects the opportunity.

Built by handlit.
